<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">D. F. Malan</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1874–1959</span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Daniel François Malan</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">D. F. Malan was a South African <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dutch">Dutch</a> Reformed minister, newspaper editor and politician who served as prime minister from 1948 to 1954. Leading the Reunited <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/national-party-south-africa">National Party</a>, he won the 1948 election on the platform of apartheid and began building it into law with the Population Registration Act, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/group-areas-act">Group Areas Act</a> and other measures. He retired in 1954 and was succeeded by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/j-g-strijdom">J. G. Strijdom</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/d-f-malan.jpg" alt="D. F. Malan" width="320" height="440" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Suidpunt · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:DFMalanPortret.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
